## What fixing the weights buys depends on how they are used

We predicted a fixed-weight datapath would be {{ sig(PRD.P15.central) }}x smaller and use
{{ sig(PRD.P16.central) }}x less energy. The synthesis says it depends:

| how the fixed weights reach the lanes | reloadable / fixed, area | reloadable / fixed, energy |
|---|---|---|
| **a lane always sees the same weight** (the same 16 weights in every block of the row, so the constants fold into the multipliers) | **{{ n(k1.area_ratio_reloadable_over_fixed.median, 2) }}** ({{ n(k1.area_ratio_reloadable_over_fixed.min, 2) }} to {{ n(k1.area_ratio_reloadable_over_fixed.max, 2) }}, n={{ k1.area_ratio_reloadable_over_fixed.n }}) | **{{ n(k1.energy_ratio_reloadable_over_fixed.median, 2) }}** ({{ n(k1.energy_ratio_reloadable_over_fixed.min, 2) }} to {{ n(k1.energy_ratio_reloadable_over_fixed.max, 2) }}) |
| **a whole row of {{ n(fix.weights_in_module) }} weights time-shared through 16 lanes** (the weights become a table in logic) | {{ n(Z.ratios_reloadable_over_fixed.cell_area.median, 2) }} | {{ n(Z.ratios_reloadable_over_fixed.energy_per_block.median, 2) }} |

- When every lane has its own fixed weight, the block shrinks by {{ n(k1.area_ratio_reloadable_over_fixed.median, 2) }}x and
  saves {{ n(k1.energy_ratio_reloadable_over_fixed.median, 2) }}x in energy. The multipliers fold away; the adder tree
  and the 32-bit accumulator remain, and they are most of the block. That is the comparison the
  predictions meant: area landed {{ PRD.P15.verdict }} the predicted range, energy {{ PRD.P16.verdict }} it.
  (A first version of this test used rows one block long. Synthesis then removed the accumulator as
  well and showed a larger saving; that result is kept in the JSON with a warning and is not used.)
- When the fixed weights are time-shared, the fixed module is *larger* than the reloadable one,
  because it contains its weights and the reloadable one does not. As storage it is dense:
  {{ n(fix.area_per_weight_um2, 2) }} µm² per hard-wired weight in standard cells, datapath included.
  For scale, the one shipped fixed-weight chip holds 8B weights on 815 mm², about
  {{ n(815e6 / 8e9, 2) }} µm² per weight on an older process with custom cells (taalas-hc1).

So fixing a lane's weight saves little in that lane. The reloadable design is still larger, for two
reasons: an SRAM bit is larger than a mask-programmed one, and S has a multiplier lane on every bank,
which is {{ pct(I.designs.s.area.mac_lanes / I.designs.s.area.silicon_mm2) }} of its silicon, while the fixed design's density figure comes from one shipped chip
with its arithmetic folded in. How much of the difference is reloadability and how much is that chip's
design cannot be separated from public data.

